      "side": 1,
      "message": "`lseq-take` (and `lseq-\u003egenerator` 105, `lseq-map` 120, `lseq-filter` 153) calls `lseq-cdr` on its source right after handing out an element, so it forces one element past what was asked for.\n\n- **Hang:** `(lseq-realize (lseq-take (lseq-filter (lambda (x) (\u003c x 3)) (generator-\u003elseq naturals)) 3))` never returns. Taking the third element makes the filter look for a fourth, which doesn\u0027t exist. `lseq-\u003egenerator` over the same filter hangs on its third call.\n- **Read-ahead:** with `cs \u003d (generator-\u003elseq (lambda () (read-char p)))` over \"abcdef\", `(lseq-realize (lseq-take cs 3))` gives `(a b c)`, but the next `(read-char p)` gives `#\\e`, not `#\\d`. `(lseq-car (lseq-map char-upcase cs))` reads two characters.\n\nThe sample has the same flaw. Advance the source at the start of the next call instead, e.g. a `started` flag and `(when started (set! lseq (lseq-cdr lseq)))` before `(lseq-car lseq)`, with `lseq-take` checking its count first. Apply that to all four and add the filter and port cases to the test.",
      "revId": "b8ac22d5b0a55b684657d0706c0efe31172e334b",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
