      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T21:38:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`vector-reverse-copy!` within one vector is wrong in three ways.\n\n- **Same start:** `(let ((v (vector 1 2 3 4 5))) (vector-reverse-copy! v 0 v 0 5) v)` gives `#(5 4 3 4 5)`, expected `#(5 4 3 2 1)`. `(vector-reverse-copy! v 1 v 1 4)` gives `#(1 4 3 4 5)`, expected `#(1 4 3 2 5)`. When tstart \u003d sstart, the check lets the call through and the loop reads elements it has already overwritten. SRFI 43 says that case behaves as `vector-reverse!`.\n- **Adjacent ranges:** on `#(1 2 3 4)`, `(vector-reverse-copy! v 2 v 0 2)` raises \"vector range for self-copying overlaps\" (expected `#(1 2 2 1)`). The bounds test uses `\u003c\u003d`.\n- **Overlapping ranges:** they raise, e.g. `(vector-reverse-copy! v 1 v 0 3)`, expected `#(1 3 2 1)`. SRFI 133 defines this procedure as \"like `vector-copy!`\", which must behave as if it copied through a temporary. The \"error if the ranges overlap\" rule is SRFI 43\u0027s.\n\nAll of this comes from the sample implementation, and the regression test never copies within one vector. Drop the overlap branch: after the range checks, `(vector-copy! target tstart source sstart send)` then `(vector-reverse! target tstart (+ tstart (- send sstart)))`. That\u0027s overlap-safe and allocates nothing. Add the cases above to the test.",
      "revId": "b8ac22d5b0a55b684657d0706c0efe31172e334b",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    }
